    {
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "Top 10 VMs by vCPU wait % (healthy < 5%, act > 10%)",
      "description": "**vCPU wait** (the kernel's measure of CPU ready) is the share of time a VM's vCPUs were ready to run but had to wait for a physical core.\nVME reports it per VM as the average across its vCPUs (for a host: across the VMs on it).\n\n| Per vCPU | What it means | What to do |\n|---|---|---|\n| **under 5%** | Healthy | Nothing |\n| **5 to 10%** | Contention: VMs are queuing for CPU | Check the host's CPU usage and vCPU:core ratio; watch the trend |\n| **over 10%** | VMs are noticeably slowed | Move VMs to a quieter host, cut vCPUs on oversized VMs, or add capacity |\n\nLook at sustained values: short spikes during backups, patching or boot storms are normal.\nToo many vCPUs per physical core raises ready time for every VM on the host, so right-size large VMs first.\n\nThis is the reference measurement; VME's own CPU ready figure tends to read higher.",
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "vme-prom"
          },
          "refId": "A",
          "expr": "topk(10, 100 * sum by (nodename, domain) (rate(libvirt_domain_vcpu_delay_seconds_total{source=~\"$source\", cluster=~\"$cluster\", nodename=~\"$host\", domain=~\"$domain\"}[$__rate_interval])) / on (nodename, domain) max by (nodename, domain) (libvirt_domain_info_virtual_cpus{source=~\"$source\", cluster=~\"$cluster\", nodename=~\"$host\", domain=~\"$domain\"}))",
          "legendFormat": "{{domain}}",
          "instant": false,
          "range": true,
          "format": "time_series",
          "editorMode": "code"
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "percent",
          "color": {
            "mode": "palette-classic"
          },
          "custom": {
            "drawStyle": "line",
            "lineWidth": 1,
            "fillOpacity": 10,
            "showPoints": "never",
            "spanNulls": 300000,
            "stacking": {
              "mode": "none"
            },
            "thresholdsStyle": {
              "mode": "line+area"
            }
          },
          "thresholds": {
            "mode": "absolute",
            "steps": [
              {
                "color": "transparent",
                "value": null
              },
              {
                "color": "#EAB839",
                "value": 5
              },
              {
                "color": "red",
                "value": 10
              }
            ]
          },
          "min": 0
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "mean",
            "max",
            "lastNotNull"
          ],
          "sortBy": "Mean",
          "sortDesc": true
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "vme-prom"
      },
      "id": 5,
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 9,
        "w": 16,
        "h": 8
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bargauge",
      "title": "vCPU wait % now (healthy < 5%)",
      "description": "**vCPU wait** (the kernel's measure of CPU ready) is the share of time a VM's vCPUs were ready to run but had to wait for a physical core.\nVME reports it per VM as the average across its vCPUs (for a host: across the VMs on it).\n\n| Per vCPU | What it means | What to do |\n|---|---|---|\n| **under 5%** | Healthy | Nothing |\n| **5 to 10%** | Contention: VMs are queuing for CPU | Check the host's CPU usage and vCPU:core ratio; watch the trend |\n| **over 10%** | VMs are noticeably slowed | Move VMs to a quieter host, cut vCPUs on oversized VMs, or add capacity |\n\nLook at sustained values: short spikes during backups, patching or boot storms are normal.\nToo many vCPUs per physical core raises ready time for every VM on the host, so right-size large VMs first.\n\nThis is the reference measurement; VME's own CPU ready figure tends to read higher.",
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "vme-prom"
          },
          "refId": "A",
          "expr": "topk(15, 100 * sum by (nodename, domain) (rate(libvirt_domain_vcpu_delay_seconds_total{source=~\"$source\", cluster=~\"$cluster\", nodename=~\"$host\", domain=~\"$domain\"}[$__rate_interval])) / on (nodename, domain) max by (nodename, domain) (libvirt_domain_info_virtual_cpus{source=~\"$source\", cluster=~\"$cluster\", nodename=~\"$host\", domain=~\"$domain\"}))",
          "legendFormat": "{{domain}}",
          "instant": true,
          "range": false,
          "format": "time_series",
          "editorMode": "code"
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "percent",
          "min": 0,
          "color": {
            "mode": "thresholds"
          },
          "thresholds": {
            "mode": "absolute",
            "steps": [
              {
                "color": "green",
                "value": null
              },
              {
                "color": "#EAB839",
                "value": 5
              },
              {
                "color": "red",
                "value": 10
              }
            ]
          },
          "max": 20
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "orientation": "horizontal",
        "displayMode": "gradient",
        "showUnfilled": true,
        "valueMode": "color",
        "namePlacement": "left",
        "sizing": "auto",
        "reduceOptions": {
          "values": false,
